#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""stop-identify — decide whether a real stop needs asking about at all.

Once a real stop (over 15 minutes) is identified, the most likely places within
30 metres are shown as a multiple choice in the chat, for the user to TAP (not
type) — but only when no single place is known with high confidence: a
repeated place, a saved favorite, or a singular place in a less dense area.

So the question is the FALLBACK, not the default. That inversion is the whole
point of this tool. The enrichment queue's previous policy was "never silently
auto-resolve, every stop is queued for the user", which is why 46 asks went out
in one week and roughly two thirds went unanswered. A question the user has
already answered — about a café visited weekly, or a place the user saved in
Google Maps — is not diligence, it is noise.

FIVE WAYS A STOP CAN NAME ITSELF, in descending strength:

  already-resolved — THIS row already carries a resolution: `place_type` is
              `user-confirmed` (the user answered a pending ask, or `findme
              resolve` was run directly) or `named` (a verified
              photo/saved-place writeback). Every other consumer of
              `location_history` already treats the row's own `place_type` as
              absolute; classify() didn't check it, so an answered stop could
              still be re-asked from scratch via a fresh Places lookup (a row
              already user-confirmed as a restaurant was tapped out as a 4-way
              choice between unrelated storefronts that didn't even include it).
  recurring — the user has CONFIRMED this same spot before. The user's own past
              answer is the strongest signal in the system; re-asking it is the
              specific failure the original complaint was about.
  favorite  — a place the user saved (the Google Maps export and any extra
              saved-places list; place_naming.load_saved merges them), within
              the same 30m. Saving it is an act of intent; thousands of them
              exist and they are the user's own labels.
  calendar  — a calendar event overlapping the stop window whose own `location`
              lies at the stop. The calendar is a clue the stop finder must use.
              Correlates on geometry, NOT on city words: an address word test
              lets a city name match every address in that city, so an unscoped
              match degrades to "any event overlapping in time."
  singular  — exactly one plausible venue within 30m. In a thin area distance
              alone identifies the stop, which is precisely NOT true in the
              dense case: one city block returned seven venues at an identical
              14.2m because Google gives one building centroid per address.
              That tie is what the tap list is for.

High confidence does NOT mean silent: even a singular location is confirmed
with the user — "Are you at <place>?" with a yes/no tappable result. So the
rungs above decide the SHAPE of the prompt, not whether there is one: a
confident stop asks one yes/no, an unclear one offers up to four choices. Both
are one tap.

A DECISIVE PROMPT CARRIES A DEFAULT, in case the user doesn't respond. Silence
is the normal case — two thirds of the asks in one week went unanswered. The
default marks the likeliest option on the card. An expired, unanswered card's
default is applied as a LOW-CONFIDENCE guess (`choice sweep --apply` → `findme
guess`): the card's own row only, as `default-applied`, never `user-confirmed`
(absolute to resolve-day, reserved for what the user actually said), never
confirmed-stops.json; the summary says "probably X" and any tap or correction
replaces it. A default must therefore stay decisive-only (`default_id: null`
otherwise), because silence now applies it.

Under 15 minutes gets nothing — that floor is `findme enrich`'s and shared.

This tool DECIDES and reports. It does not send, and it does not write. Wiring
the tap UI is a client change; seeing the decisions is worth having first.

Config — agent/local/config/stop-identify.json (optional):
  lodging_label  the name a one-tap "were you at …?" uses for a place the user
                 sleeps at that nothing names yet (default "the rental")

Usage:
  stop-identify <YYYY-MM-DD> [--tz Europe/Paris] [--json]
  stop-identify --visit <uuid>
"""
import argparse
import json
import math
import os
import re
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request
from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone
from zoneinfo import ZoneInfo

HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
sys.path.insert(0, os.path.join(os.path.dirname(HERE), "lib"))
import places_local  # noqa: E402
import place_naming as _place_naming  # noqa: E402

REPO = places_local.install_root()
ENV_FILE = os.path.join(REPO, ".env")
SUPABASE_URL = _place_naming.SUPABASE_URL  # SUPABASE_URL, else the instance's supabase_project
SAVED_PLACES = places_local.data_path("google-saved-places.json")
CFG = places_local.local_config("stop-identify", {"lodging_label": "the rental"})

# Both numbers deliberate. 15 min is already `findme enrich`'s
# min_duration_min — a shorter cluster is a red light or a dog's sniff, and the
# user cannot reliably recall it. 30m is tighter than the 60m used for reporting
# candidates, because a tap list must be things the user could plausibly have
# been INSIDE, not things that were merely nearby.
MIN_STOP_MIN = 15.0
ASK_RADIUS_M = 30.0
# How far a past confirmation still counts as "this same spot". Wider than the
# ask radius on purpose: the anchors of two visits to one café differ by GPS
# noise, not by the user having gone somewhere else.
# 25m, not 60m. 60 was chosen to absorb GPS noise between two visits to one
# venue, but on a dense street it also swallows the NEIGHBOURS: one café's
# anchor found prior confirmations for two other restaurants inside 60m, so the names disagreed and the rung could never fire — the exact
# stop it was meant to recognise. 25m is about one shopfront, which is the unit
# this rung is actually claiming.
RECUR_RADIUS_M = 25.0
RECUR_LOOKBACK_DAYS = 180
MAX_CHOICES = 4

# The far end of a drive out and back: driving to a place and returning without
# a stop makes the turning-around point a destination worth asking about, if
# the purpose of the drive is not already known. bin/day-segments §
# turnaround_stops writes the row and marks its notes with this token — a
# string WE generate, matched by membership, never read from the user.
# The floor is findme resolve's own 8 minutes: a window it would refuse to name
# is one the question could never land on.
TURNAROUND_TOKEN = "[turnaround]"
TURNAROUND_MIN_MIN = 8.0
# The row's position is the mean of the fixes near the far point, and at
# five-minute cadence in a car those spread over kilometres (an airport run
# sat 1.7 km from its terminal), so the shortlist reaches wide.
TURNAROUND_PLACE_RADIUS_M = 2500

# Places will happily return the ADDRESS ITSELF as a result. The tappable
# options beside a restaurant once included the street address of the stop —
# offering the user's own coordinates back as an answer to where the user was,
# which is the question restated, not a choice. Filtered by TYPE rather
# than by how the string looks: a geocode result is identifiable from its type
# list, and pattern-matching the text would be guessing at language.
ADDRESS_TYPES = {
    "street_address", "route", "premise", "subpremise", "intersection",
    "postal_code", "postal_code_prefix", "plus_code", "geocode",
    "neighborhood", "locality", "sublocality", "political", "administrative_area_level_1",
}


# Where a 15-minute stop plausibly happens, and where it plausibly does not.
# "wholesale"/"warehouse_store": a warehouse club's own OSM tag and Google
# Places' "New" API type respectively — missing both is why a genuine 49-minute
# warehouse-club stop ranked no better than "other" (prior 1) instead of the
# destination prior it obviously deserves.
# The stored value of every "not one of these" button. A protocol token WE
# define — never a place — so the tap fast path (lib/choice-reply.mjs) can hand
# it to the agent and `findme resolve` can refuse it by set membership over our
# own strings, without reading the user's language.
OTHER_VALUE = "__other__"

DESTINATION_TYPES = {
    "restaurant", "cafe", "coffee_shop", "bakery", "bar", "wine_bar", "pub",
    "meal_takeaway", "food", "ice_cream_shop", "book_store", "clothing_store",
    "store", "supermarket", "grocery_store", "department_store", "museum",
    "art_gallery", "tourist_attraction", "park", "movie_theater", "gym",
    "french_restaurant", "pizza_restaurant", "sandwich_shop",
    "wholesale", "warehouse_store",
}
SERVICE_TYPES = {
    "laundry", "dry_cleaner", "hair_salon", "beauty_salon", "government_office",
    "medical_clinic", "doctor", "dentist", "veterinary_care", "health",
    "insurance_agency", "accounting", "lawyer", "school", "primary_school",
    "educational_institution", "catering_service", "florist", "pharmacy",
}

# A default is only applied on silence when the ranking is DECISIVE — the top
# option must beat the runner-up on the destination prior, or be materially
# nearer. Otherwise there is no defensible default and silence records nothing.
# Writing a dry cleaner or a nursery school into the user's day because the
# user was busy is worse than leaving the stop unresolved: an unresolved stop is
# visibly unanswered, a wrong one reads as fact.

# Not somewhere the user "stopped" in the sense the question means — mirrors
# visit-anchor's list, for the same reason: short-let flats are indexed by the
# dozen on a central street and would fill a tap list with noise.
DEMOTED_TYPES = {
    "lodging", "apartment_complex", "apartment_building", "real_estate_agency",
    "general_contractor", "bus_stop", "transit_station", "subway_station",
    "train_station", "parking", "atm", "storage", "moving_company",
    "private_guest_room",
}


def load_key():
    return _place_naming.load_key() or sys.exit("no SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY")


def _get(path, key):
    if not SUPABASE_URL:
        sys.exit("no Supabase project configured (SUPABASE_URL or instance supabase_project)")
    req = urllib.request.Request(f"{SUPABASE_URL}/rest/v1/{path}",
                                 headers={"apikey": key, "Authorization": f"Bearer {key}"})
    with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=45) as r:
        return json.loads(r.read())


def meters(la1, lo1, la2, lo2):
    return math.hypot((la2 - la1) * 111320.0,
                      (lo2 - lo1) * 111320.0 * math.cos(math.radians((la1 + la2) / 2)))


# `load_saved` and `prior_confirmations` live in lib/place_naming.py. They
# were lifted verbatim, not rewritten: `findme enrich` needs
# the SAME two rungs to decide what enters the ask queue, and two copies of
# "have we been here before" would drift the first time either radius was
# tuned. This file still owns the DECISION -- which rung fires, whether to ask,
# and what the default is; the module only reports what the history says.
from place_naming import (  # noqa: E402
    load_saved, prior_confirmations, match_named_location, NAMED_LOCATION_MATCH_M,
    scheduled_lodging, repeat_stay_name, match_named_location_by_address,
    scheduled_lodging_stay, match_booking_by_address,
)




# A stay past this is not a visit to a business. Set well clear of a long
# restaurant lunch (around 45 min is typical) so that only somewhere the user
# stayed the night can trip it.
SLEEP_STAY_HOURS = 6.0
SLEEP_MIN_NIGHTS = 2


def sleeps_here(lat, lon, key, exclude_id=None):
    """Nights slept here, and any names confirmed ON those long stays.

    Returns (nights, names). The names matter as much as the count: a name
    confirmed for a 20-minute coffee says nothing about where the user SLEEPS,
    and treating it as the lodging's name is how a 65-minute stay at a rented
    flat was about to be recorded, silently and without a question, as a café
    the user had once confirmed 25m away.
    """
    since = (datetime.now(timezone.utc)
             - timedelta(days=RECUR_LOOKBACK_DAYS)).isoformat()
    dlat = RECUR_RADIUS_M / 111320.0
    dlon = RECUR_RADIUS_M / (111320.0 * max(math.cos(math.radians(lat)), 1e-6))
    rows = _get(
        "location_history?segment_type=eq.visit&departed_at=not.is.null"
        f"&arrived_at=gte.{urllib.parse.quote(since)}"
        f"&latitude=gte.{lat - dlat}&latitude=lte.{lat + dlat}"
        f"&longitude=gte.{lon - dlon}&longitude=lte.{lon + dlon}"
        "&select=id,latitude,longitude,arrived_at,departed_at,place_name,place_type"
        "&limit=500", key) or []
    nights, names = set(), []
    for r in rows:
        if r["id"] == exclude_id:
            continue
        if meters(lat, lon, r["latitude"], r["longitude"]) > RECUR_RADIUS_M:
            continue
        a = datetime.fromisoformat(r["arrived_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        d = datetime.fromisoformat(r["departed_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        if (d - a).total_seconds() / 3600 < SLEEP_STAY_HOURS:
            continue
        nights.add(a.date())
        if r.get("place_type") == "user-confirmed" and r.get("place_name"):
            names.append(r["place_name"].strip())
    return len(nights), list(dict.fromkeys(names))


# ── A night at the booked lodging ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A night's sleep at the booked rental must never trigger a question, yet one
# did: "Looking back to this morning, around 02:43 — 02:43–09:56 (433 min) …
# which of these was it?" (a café, a bike rental). Every lodging fact was on
# record: the booking (travel_segments, a month-long stay), five nights within
# 25 m of the visit row, and three answers naming the rental at the same spot.
# None was read, because rest_anchor had moved the stop 38 m to where the trace
# rested longest: 53.6 min of fixes at 09:00–09:56, the café, while the six
# hours of sleep left four fixes. A sleeping phone is silent (location-spine),
# so on a night the longest rest is the hour he was awake. From the café no
# night was within 25 m, and the stop fell through to Places.
#
# Two changes. A long stay is measured from its row unless its longest rest is
# at least NIGHT_REST_SHARE of it. And a stay of SLEEP_STAY_HOURS or more, or
# one covering NIGHT_HOURS, on a booked date is the booking, asked about never,
# when the user slept within LODGING_MATCH_M of the row OR the rest point on another
# night of that same booking, or (the first night, when there is no other) when
# a model reads the stop's street address as the booking's. 150 m is the reach
# of a home in named-locations (NAMED_LOCATION_MATCH_M): a month's night rows
# at one flat sit mostly 0–80 m apart, so 25 m was too tight for a bed.
LODGING_MATCH_M = 150.0
NIGHT_REST_SHARE = 0.5
# The small hours, local. Another stay counts as a NIGHT only if it covers part
# of them: two long days at a friend's or a co-working space 100 m from the flat
# are not where the user sleeps. A stop that covers them is checked even when
# the phone split the night into pieces under SLEEP_STAY_HOURS. Sleep is
# typically ~1–9am.
NIGHT_HOURS = (2, 6)


def overlaps_night(start, end, tz):
    """Does [start, end) cover any of NIGHT_HOURS in `tz`? None without a tz."""
    if tz is None:
        return None
    day = start.astimezone(tz).date() - timedelta(days=1)
    while day <= end.astimezone(tz).date():
        w0 = datetime(day.year, day.month, day.day, NIGHT_HOURS[0], tzinfo=tz)
        w1 = datetime(day.year, day.month, day.day, NIGHT_HOURS[1], tzinfo=tz)
        if start < w1 and w0 < end:
            return True
        day += timedelta(days=1)
    return False


def booked_nights(key, booking, stay_start, stay_end, tz=None, near=None):
    """OTHER nights of this booking, as [(date, lat, lon)]. A row overlapping
    this stay is this stay — the client's duplicate rows carry different ids —
    so overlap excludes it, not its id. With a tz, a night must also cover
    NIGHT_HOURS. `near` (a list of points) narrows the query to
    LODGING_MATCH_M around them; without it, every night of the booking."""
    since = booking["arrive_at"]
    until = booking.get("depart_at") or datetime.now(timezone.utc)
    box = ""
    if near:
        lats = [p[0] for p in near]
        lons = [p[1] for p in near]
        dlat = LODGING_MATCH_M / 111320.0
        dlon = LODGING_MATCH_M / (111320.0 * max(math.cos(math.radians(max(abs(x) for x in lats))), 1e-6))
        box = (f"&latitude=gte.{min(lats) - dlat}&latitude=lte.{max(lats) + dlat}"
               f"&longitude=gte.{min(lons) - dlon}&longitude=lte.{max(lons) + dlon}")
    rows = _get(
        "location_history?segment_type=eq.visit&departed_at=not.is.null"
        f"&arrived_at=gte.{urllib.parse.quote(since.isoformat())}"
        f"&arrived_at=lte.{urllib.parse.quote(until.isoformat())}"
        f"{box}&select=id,latitude,longitude,arrived_at,departed_at&order=arrived_at.desc&limit=1000", key) or []
    nights = []
    for r in rows:
        try:
            a = datetime.fromisoformat(r["arrived_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00"))
            d = datetime.fromisoformat(r["departed_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00"))
        except (KeyError, TypeError, ValueError, AttributeError):
            continue
        if r.get("latitude") is None or r.get("longitude") is None:
            continue
        if a < stay_end and stay_start < d:
            continue
        if (d - a).total_seconds() / 3600 < SLEEP_STAY_HOURS:
            continue
        if tz is not None and not overlaps_night(a, d, tz):
            continue
        nights.append((a.date(), r["latitude"], r["longitude"]))
    return nights


def booked_nights_near(points, key, booking, stay_start, stay_end, tz=None):
    """Dates of OTHER nights of this booking the user slept within LODGING_MATCH_M of
    any of `points`."""
    return {day for day, la, lo in booked_nights(key, booking, stay_start, stay_end, tz, near=points)
            if min(meters(pla, plo, la, lo) for pla, plo in points) <= LODGING_MATCH_M}


def overnight_at_booking(visit, arr, dep, tz, key, points):
    """{name, reason} when this night stay is the booked lodging, else None.
    Any failure returns None, and the ladder carries on as before."""
    try:
        booking = scheduled_lodging_stay((arr.astimezone(tz) if tz else arr).date())
        if not booking:
            return None
        hours = (dep - arr).total_seconds() / 3600
        nights = booked_nights_near(points, key, booking, arr, dep, tz)
        if nights:
            return {"name": booking["name"],
                    "reason": f"a {hours:.1f}h stay within {LODGING_MATCH_M:.0f}m of where the user slept "
                              f"on {len(nights)} other night(s) of the booking at {booking['name']}"}
        labels = [visit.get("place_name")] if visit.get("place_type") == "address" else []
        lat, lon = points[0]
        if match_booking_by_address(lat, lon, booking["name"], labels):
            return {"name": booking["name"],
                    "reason": f"a {hours:.1f}h stay whose street address is the booking's "
                              f"({booking['name']}); the first night there"}
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[stop-identify] overnight lodging check failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    return None


# ── The booked lodging as an answer to offer ─────────────────────────────
#
# On the same question the rental was not even one of the options. No list
# could have held it. The booking has a label and no coordinates, and Places
# never returns a private flat (a `lodging` type is demoted when it does), so a
# stop by the flat that missed every silent rung was offered the café
# downstairs and a bike rental. Another 41-minute stop 30 m from the flat went
# out as four unrelated businesses, and the user typed the rental's name.
#
# So the booking is placed from the user's own nights there: the median point
# of the booking's other nights (6 h+, covering NIGHT_HOURS). On the case that
# built it, that median held 52 of a month's 57 visit rows named for the flat
# within 45 m (the other five are stray fixes 160 m or more out), with one café
# at 22 m and another at 62 m: hence LODGING_NEAR_M for a day stop, which GPS
# cannot separate from the flat, while a night keeps the 150 m of
# LODGING_MATCH_M. Nights placing it farther away mean the user was not there. With
# no other night yet, a whole night is the booking's by its dates alone, and a
# day stop is placed by its street address (address-match --booking, a model).
#
# It only joins a question that was going to be asked: a stop the ladder names
# silently stays silent. The one exception is a night that only the density
# rung would name (a lone café within 30 m), because a café list is the wrong
# question about a night: it becomes one tap, "were you at <booking>?",
# defaulting to yes. The user's own answers and a bed slept in still come first.
LODGING_NEAR_M = 50.0


def booked_lodging_option(visit, arr, dep, tz, key, points, at, night):
    """{name, reason} when the booked lodging belongs among this stop's
    answers, else None. `at` is the stop's anchor, `night` whether the stay is
    one. Any failure returns None, and the question is built as before."""
    try:
        booking = scheduled_lodging_stay((arr.astimezone(tz) if tz else arr).date())
        if not booking:
            return None
        # The lookup is by date; the stay must also fall inside the booking's
        # own window (a night at the airport before check-in is not the flat).
        if dep <= booking["arrive_at"] or (booking.get("depart_at") and arr >= booking["depart_at"]):
            return None
        name = booking["name"]
        nights = booked_nights(key, booking, arr, dep, tz)
        if nights:
            mid = (statistics.median(n[1] for n in nights), statistics.median(n[2] for n in nights))
            d = min(meters(la, lo, mid[0], mid[1]) for la, lo in points)
            if d > (LODGING_MATCH_M if night else LODGING_NEAR_M):
                return None
            return {"name": name,
                    "reason": f"{d:.0f}m from the middle of {len(nights)} other night(s) at {name}"}
        if night:
            # The night rung has already read the address. With nothing to
            # place the booking, only a whole night is its by the dates alone:
            # six hours that cover the small hours. A 01:30 stop at the airport
            # on arrival, or a long first day elsewhere, is not.
            if (dep - arr).total_seconds() / 3600 >= SLEEP_STAY_HOURS and overlaps_night(arr, dep, tz) is not False:
                return {"name": name,
                        "reason": f"a whole night on the dates booked at {name}, and no other night there yet"}
            return None
        labels = [visit.get("place_name")] if visit.get("place_type") == "address" else []
        if match_booking_by_address(at[0], at[1], name, labels):
            return {"name": name,
                    "reason": f"its street address is the booking's ({name}); no night there yet"}
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[stop-identify] lodging option check failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
    return None


def _lodging_choice(stay):
    """The tap option for the booked lodging. The label is the booking's own
    name, so a tap stores the name the lodging rungs already use."""
    return {"id": "__lodging__", "label": stay["name"], "sublabel": "where you're staying"}


# Above this we do not ask at all: the question is only for a place not known
# with a very high certainty (>80%). A question whose answer we can already
# predict four times in five is not diligence, it is interruption — the same
# complaint that produced 46 asks in one week with two thirds unanswered.
CERTAINTY_SKIP = 0.80

# What each rung is worth. Deliberately explicit rather than emergent: these are
# claims about how often each signal is RIGHT, and writing them as numbers is
# what lets the 80% line mean something.
CONFIDENCE = {
    "already-resolved": 0.99,  # this exact row already carries the user's own answer
    "named_location":  0.97,  # the user's own home/family address — definitional, not inferred
    "recurring":       0.95,  # the user's own past answer, same spot, no disagreement
    "repeat-stay":     0.90,  # one past answer about a stay, and this is a stay there too
    "lodging-booked":  0.92,  # the user sleeps at this spot, and the trip's booking names it
    "lodging-known":   0.90,  # we already have a confirmed name for where the user sleeps
    "favorite":        0.85,  # the single place the user saved within 30m
    "calendar":        0.88,  # a calendar event overlapping the window, address-correlated
    "singular":        0.82,  # the only venue at all within 30m
    "lodging-night":   0.75,  # a night on the booked dates; nothing yet places the booking
    "lodging-inferred": 0.70,  # long stays say the user sleeps here; nothing says what it IS
}


def _resolve(out, name, kind, reason):
    """Skip or ask, on the 80% line — never a silent write below it.

    A rung firing is not the same as being sure. `recurring` is the user's own
    word played back and needs no confirmation; `lodging-inferred` knows only
    that the user sleeps somewhere, not what to call it, so it asks — one tap, defaulting to
    yes, because the evidence does point that way.
    """
    conf = CONFIDENCE[kind]
    out.update(confidence=kind, certainty=conf, name=name, reason=reason)
    if conf >= CERTAINTY_SKIP:
        out.update(verdict="skip-known")
        return out
    out.update(verdict="confirm", question=f"Were you at {name}?",
               prompt=((lambda c: f"{c} — were you at {name}?" if c else f"Were you at {name}?")(out.get("_ctx") or "")),
               # `value` is what gets STORED; `label` is only what the button
               # reads. Without the split the tap text itself became the place
               # name — two rows were literally called "Yes — the rental", and
               # the confirmed rung then reused that as the name of the user's
               # home for every later stop there.
               # The "no" button carries the protocol value OTHER_VALUE, never
               # its label: when it had no value, a tap reached `findme
               # resolve` as the text "No, somewhere else" and was saved as a
               # user-confirmed place name. choice-reply hands OTHER_VALUE to
               # the agent (the user is asked where, in their own words) and
               # `findme resolve` refuses to store it.
               options=[{"id": "__yes__", "label": f"Yes — {name}", "value": name},
                        {"id": "__no__", "label": "No, somewhere else", "value": OTHER_VALUE}],
               default_id="__yes__")
    return out


def _context(visit, arr, dep, tz, prev):
    """The sentence that makes the question answerable.

    A bare "where was this?" is not a question the user can answer: a
    question must carry the detail — time, where the user was before,
    approximate location, duration. By the time a prompt lands the user may be
    two stops further on, and "where was this?" asks the user to reconstruct
    which unnamed gap we mean before even considering the options. Everything below is already in the row we are asking about — it
    cost nothing to withhold it, and withholding it made the question useless.
    """
    bits = []
    if tz and dep:
        t0, t1 = arr.astimezone(tz), dep.astimezone(tz)
        mins = round((dep - arr).total_seconds() / 60)
        same_day = t0.date() == datetime.now(tz).date()
        day = "" if same_day else f"{t0:%a} "
        bits.append(f"{day}{t0:%H:%M}–{t1:%H:%M} ({mins} min)")
    addr = (visit.get("address") or "").strip()
    if addr:
        # Street-level is the useful grain; the city and country are noise to
        # someone standing in it.
        bits.append(f"around {addr.split(',')[0].strip()}")
    # A previous "name" is only useful as a landmark if it reads like one. Some
    # confirmations are whole typed sentences ("Stopped for a few minutes to
    # leave a review, then continued on"), and pasting that into the
    # next question makes it harder to read, not easier. Judged on LENGTH, which
    # is a property of the field, not on what the words mean.
    if prev and len(prev) <= 40:
        bits.append(f"after {prev}")
    return ", ".join(bits)


def _osm_fallback_candidates(lat, lon, radius_m):
    """OSM candidates for rung 4, used only when Google's Places lookup came
    back empty.

    Two separate reasons Google can come back empty: `places_nearby.nearby()`
    fails CLOSED to `[]` whenever the Jori Places budget is blocked
    (`lib/places_guard.py`) — which it has been for days at a time — and
    even unblocked, a
    large-footprint venue can sit outside ASK_RADIUS_M by centroid distance
    while the anchor is genuinely inside its real boundary.

    `osm_places.py` (already relied on by `day-segments`) fixes both: it is a
    second, independent source, and it carries real polygons rather than a
    pin. Verified against a supermarket and a warehouse-club stop this rung
    missed: Google returned nothing for either (budget-blocked), but OSM found
    both, and each one's own polygon CONTAINS the visit anchor — at 44m and
    83m centroid distance respectively — despite sitting outside the 30m flat
    radius Google's call uses. Containment is what actually answers "was the
    user inside this place", not distance to a pin.
    """
    try:
        import osm_places
    except Exception:
        return []
    try:
        places = osm_places.nearby(lat, lon, radius=max(radius_m, 100))
    except Exception:
        return []
    out = []
    for p in places:
        if p["category"] == "demoted":
            continue
        inside = osm_places.contains(p, lat, lon)
        d = osm_places.meters((lat, lon), (p["lat"], p["lon"]))
        if not inside and d > radius_m:
            continue
        out.append({
            "name": p["name"],
            "distance_m": 0.0 if inside else d,
            "types": [p["kind"]] if p["kind"] else [],
            "primary_type": p["kind"] or "",
            "type_display": (p["kind"] or "").replace("_", " "),
            "reviews": 0,
            "place_id": p["osm_id"],
        })
    return out


# Calendar as a clue. Every OTHER signal in this file is about GPS density;
# this is the one signal that comes from the user's own stated intent instead. `jobs/location-enrich.md`'s older
# free-text ask already does this (Signal 1: Calendar), but that playbook logic
# only exists for a human agent reading it each run — `stop-identify` runs
# headless off a cron and had no equivalent.
#
# This once correlated shared address WORDS (a stopword set copied
# from location-confirm-resolve.mjs). It is distance now — see _calendar_hint.
CALENDAR_AT = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "calendar-at")


def _calendar_hint(lat, lon, arr, dep):
    """The calendar event the user was AT during the stop, or None.

    Geometry: the event's own `location` is geocoded (cached) and must lie
    within 150 m of the stop (lib/calendar-place.mjs). The old test — a shared
    address WORD between event and stop — missed a print shop on a boulevard
    named after a city, and would have matched every event in that city to it.
    Padding stays tight (10 min), for the reason below: an all-day or long
    block overlapping the stop is not evidence the user was at it. A failed lookup is printed, never read
    as "nothing on the calendar".
    """
    if lat is None or lon is None:
        return None
    pad = timedelta(minutes=10)
    try:
        res = subprocess.run(
            [CALENDAR_AT, "--start", (arr - pad).isoformat(), "--end", (dep + pad).isoformat(),
             "--point", f"{lat},{lon}", "--json"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=60)
        out = json.loads(res.stdout or "{}")
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[stop-identify] calendar-at failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    if out.get("error"):
        print(f"[stop-identify] calendar lookup failed: {out['error']}", file=sys.stderr)
    # The venue at the event's LOCATION — never the event title, which is only
    # a hint (the event was named for the errand; the shop had its own name). A dict:
    # {name, source, shortlist?}; `calendar_ambiguous` carries no name.
    return out.get("venue") or None


# A long stop at a mealtime is a meal. Places types are machine ids, so this is
# set membership over Google's vocabulary, not a reading of anything the user
# said. A 66-minute stop from 14:24, where "store" (a tablecloth shop, 3 m)
# ranked level with a 3,264-review grill, so distance decided it.
MEAL_MIN = 30.0
FOOD_TYPES = {"restaurant", "meal_takeaway", "meal_delivery", "food", "food_court",
              "diner", "brunch_restaurant", "breakfast_restaurant", "bar_and_grill"}
CAFE_TYPES = {"cafe", "coffee_shop", "bakery", "breakfast_restaurant", "brunch_restaurant"}


def _meal_kinds(arr, mins, tz):
    if not mins or mins < MEAL_MIN or tz is None:
        return None
    local = arr.astimezone(tz)
    h = local.hour + local.minute / 60
    if 7 <= h < 10.75:
        return CAFE_TYPES
    if 11.5 <= h < 15.5 or 18 <= h < 22.5:
        return FOOD_TYPES
    return None


def _serves(types, kinds):
    return bool(types & kinds) or (kinds is FOOD_TYPES and any(t.endswith("_restaurant") for t in types))


def rest_anchor(visit, arr, dep):
    """Where the user SAT, from the raw trace (bin/rest-point), or None.

    The row's lat/lon is a centroid. A 66-minute lunch spread 205 m; its
    centroid sat 101 m from the table, the 30 m circle around it missed the
    restaurant 20 m from the rest, and the user was asked to pick between
    a tablecloth shop and a children's outlet. Every proximity rung below
    measures from this point when the trace has one."""
    cmd = [os.path.join(HERE, "rest-point"), "--visit", str(visit["id"]),
           "--start", arr.isoformat()]
    if dep:
        cmd += ["--end", dep.isoformat()]
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=40)
        out = json.loads((r.stdout or "").strip().splitlines()[-1])
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"[stop-identify] rest-point failed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return None
    if out.get("lat") is None:
        return None
    return out


# What a drive out and back is most often FOR: somewhere people are taken to or
# collected from, ahead of the shops and cafés a 15-minute stop usually is. An
# airport 2 km off the mean of the fixes would otherwise lose to every café.
TURNAROUND_MAJOR_TYPES = {
    "airport", "train_station", "transit_station", "subway_station", "bus_station",
    "shopping_mall", "hospital", "university", "stadium", "tourist_attraction",
    "museum", "amusement_park", "zoo", "campus", "park",
}
# Stations are fine as a destination here; they are only noise for a 15-minute
# stop on a street.
TURNAROUND_DEMOTED_TYPES = DEMOTED_TYPES - {"train_station", "transit_station", "subway_station"}


def _ask_turnaround(out, lat, lon, ctx):
    """The question for the far end of a drive the user turned around on.

    No default: an unanswered card's default is applied as a guess, and a guess
    at where a 2 km-smeared point was is worse than leaving it open.
    """
    try:
        from places_nearby import nearby
        cands = nearby(lat, lon, radius=TURNAROUND_PLACE_RADIUS_M, max_results=20)
    except Exception:
        cands = []
    if not cands:
        cands = _osm_fallback_candidates(lat, lon, TURNAROUND_PLACE_RADIUS_M)
    plausible = [c for c in cands
                 if not ((set(c.get("types") or []) | {c.get("primary_type") or ""})
                         & (TURNAROUND_DEMOTED_TYPES | ADDRESS_TYPES))]

    def prior(c):
        t = set(c.get("types") or []) | {c.get("primary_type") or ""}
        if t & TURNAROUND_MAJOR_TYPES:
            return 0
        return 1 if t & DESTINATION_TYPES else 2
    plausible.sort(key=lambda c: (prior(c), -(c.get("reviews") or 0), c["distance_m"]))
    opts = [{"id": c.get("place_id") or c["name"], "label": c["name"],
             "sublabel": f"{c['distance_m'] / 1000:.1f} km · {c.get('type_display') or c.get('primary_type') or ''}".strip(" ·"),
             "distance_m": round(c["distance_m"])}
            for c in plausible[:MAX_CHOICES - 1]]
    opts.append({"id": "__other__", "label": "Somewhere else", "value": OTHER_VALUE,
                 "sublabel": "type it instead"})
    opts.append({"id": "__passing__", "label": "Just driving", "value": "not a stop",
                 "sublabel": "no destination"})
    lead = "you drove out this way and turned around without a stop I could see. Where were you headed?"
    out.update(verdict="choose", question="Where were you headed?",
               prompt=f"{ctx} — {lead}" if ctx else lead.capitalize(),
               options=opts, default_id=None,
               reason="the far end of a drive out and back; the purpose is not on the user's calendar or in their words "
                      f"(stop-passage checked first); {len(plausible)} place(s) within {TURNAROUND_PLACE_RADIUS_M} m")
    return out


def classify(visit, key, saved, tz=None, prev=None):
    lat, lon = visit["latitude"], visit["longitude"]
    arr = datetime.fromisoformat(visit["arrived_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    dep = (datetime.fromisoformat(visit["departed_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00"))
           if visit.get("departed_at") else None)
    mins = (dep - arr).total_seconds() / 60 if dep else None
    out = {"id": visit["id"], "arrived_at": visit["arrived_at"],
           "minutes": round(mins, 1) if mins else None,
           "stored_name": visit.get("place_name")}

    ctx = _context(visit, arr, dep, tz, prev)
    out["_ctx"] = ctx
    # The same context WITHOUT the time, for the client card, which draws the
    # time itself (relative to when the user reads it) above the map.
    out["where"] = _context(visit, arr, None, None, prev)
    out["lat"], out["lon"] = lat, lon
    out["departed_at"] = visit.get("departed_at")

    turnaround = TURNAROUND_TOKEN in (visit.get("notes") or "")
    floor = TURNAROUND_MIN_MIN if turnaround else MIN_STOP_MIN
    if mins is None or mins < floor:
        out["verdict"] = "below-floor"
        out["reason"] = f"{mins:.0f} min < {floor:.0f} min floor" if mins else "still open"
        return out

    # -1. Already resolved on THIS row. `findme enrich`'s older queue refuses
    # to even queue a visit whose own place_type is already `named` /
    # `user-confirmed` / `non-stop` (RESOLVED set in `findme`) — this tap flow
    # had no equivalent, so a row that already carries the user's own answer (or a
    # verified photo/saved-place writeback) still fell through to a fresh
    # Places lookup and got asked about again as if nothing were known. Every
    # other consumer of `location_history` — `resolve-day`, `live-day`,
    # `stop-name-writeback` — already treats `place_type` on the row itself as
    # absolute; classify() is the one place that wasn't reading it. (A visit
    # already user-confirmed as a restaurant was still asked as a 4-way tap
    # between unrelated storefronts that didn't even include it.)
    ptype = visit.get("place_type")
    pname = (visit.get("place_name") or "").strip()
    if ptype in ("user-confirmed", "named") and pname:
        return _resolve(out, pname, "already-resolved",
                        f"row already {ptype} as {pname!r}")

    # 0. Named location — the user's own home or a family address. This has to
    # come before every other rung, including `recurring`: `recurring` needs two
    # PAST confirmations at the same spot to fire, so the very first stop this
    # system ever sees at the user's own home has no history to draw on and
    # falls straight through to Google-Places density — which is exactly what
    # put a doctor's office and a media company on the lock screen for a
    # 20-minute stop that never left the apartment. A home address needs no
    # evidence; it is definitional.
    rest = rest_anchor(visit, arr, dep)
    # Both are positions of this stay; the overnight rung below reads both.
    points = [(lat, lon)] + ([(rest["lat"], rest["lon"])] if rest else [])
    long_stay = mins >= SLEEP_STAY_HOURS * 60
    if rest and long_stay and (rest.get("rest_min") or 0) < mins * NIGHT_REST_SHARE:
        # A night is not its waking hour (see LODGING_MATCH_M):
        # a 54-min rest out of 433 min is a slice of the stay, not where it was.
        out["anchor"] = {"source": "row", "rest_ignored": {
            "rest_min": rest.get("rest_min"), "moved_from_row_m": rest.get("moved_m")}}
    elif rest:
        lat, lon = rest["lat"], rest["lon"]
        out["lat"], out["lon"] = lat, lon
        out["anchor"] = {"source": "rest", "rest_min": rest.get("rest_min"),
                         "moved_from_row_m": rest.get("moved_m")}
    else:
        out["anchor"] = {"source": "row"}

    named = match_named_location(lat, lon)
    if named:
        loc, d = named
        return _resolve(out, loc["name"], "named_location",
                        f"within {NAMED_LOCATION_MATCH_M:.0f}m of {loc['name']} ({d:.0f}m)")
    # ...or the same home by its street address (a transliterated street name
    # and a house number a few doors off are still that home). A model reads the
    # addresses, in bin/address-match; the row's own label goes along only when
    # the iOS geocoder wrote it (place_type 'address'), never when it is the
    # user's answer.
    row_label = [visit.get("place_name")] if visit.get("place_type") == "address" else []
    by_addr = match_named_location_by_address(lat, lon, row_label)
    if by_addr:
        return _resolve(out, by_addr["name"], "named_location",
                        f"the same home as {by_addr['address']} by its street address")
    # A home the user drove to is a known destination, so the two checks above come
    # first; anything else at the far end of a drive is asked about wide.
    if turnaround:
        return _ask_turnaround(out, lat, lon, ctx)

    # The booked lodging as an answer to offer, looked up at most once and only
    # where a question is about to be built (see LODGING_NEAR_M).
    is_night = long_stay or bool(overlaps_night(arr, dep, tz))
    _stay = []
    def lodging():
        if not _stay:
            _stay.append(booked_lodging_option(visit, arr, dep, tz, key, points, (lat, lon), is_night))
        return _stay[0]

    # 0.5. A night at the booked lodging. After the user's own homes — a night
    # at a family home is theirs, whatever is booked — and before every venue
    # rung, because no café holds anyone six hours and a café's name at a
    # waking-hour rest must not name the night. See LODGING_MATCH_M.
    if is_night:
        night = overnight_at_booking(visit, arr, dep, tz, key, points)
        if night:
            return _resolve(out, night["name"], "lodging-booked", night["reason"])

    # 1. Repeated place — the user's own past answer outranks anything Google says.
    names = prior_confirmations(lat, lon, key, exclude_id=visit["id"])
    distinct = list(dict.fromkeys(names))
    if len(distinct) == 1 and len(names) >= 2:
        return _resolve(out, distinct[0], "recurring",
                        f"confirmed {len(names)}x at this spot, always the same name")
    # A second STAY at a spot named once is remembered, not re-asked: asked
    # about once and then remembered. Both visits
    # must be stays; see place_naming.repeat_stay_name for the guards.
    rep = (repeat_stay_name(lat, lon, key, mins, stop_arrived=visit["arrived_at"], exclude_id=visit["id"])
           if len(names) == 1 else None)
    if rep:
        return _resolve(out, rep["name"], "repeat-stay",
                        f"named once before, on a {rep['minutes']:.0f}-min stay "
                        f"{(rep.get('arrived_at') or '')[:10]}; this is a second stay")
    if len(distinct) > 1:
        out["conflicting_prior_names"] = distinct[:4]

    # 2. Favorite — the user saved it.
    near_saved = sorted(
        ((meters(lat, lon, p["lat"], p["lng"]), p) for p in saved
         if abs(p["lat"] - lat) < 0.001 and abs(p["lng"] - lon) < 0.002),
        key=lambda x: x[0])
    near_saved = [(d, p) for d, p in near_saved if d <= ASK_RADIUS_M]
    if len(near_saved) == 1:
        d, p = near_saved[0]
        return _resolve(out, p["name"], "favorite",
                        f"the only saved place within {ASK_RADIUS_M:.0f}m "
                        f"({d:.0f}m, list: {p.get('list') or '?'})")

    # 2.5. Calendar — the user's own calendar says where they'd be. Checked
    # after favorite (a saved place is still the user's stronger label) and before
    # lodging/density (where GPS is doing all the work). See `_calendar_hint`
    # for why this correlates on address tokens rather than trusting any
    # time-overlapping event.
    cal = _calendar_hint(lat, lon, arr, dep)
    if cal and cal.get("source") == "calendar_ambiguous" and cal.get("shortlist"):
        # The user was at the address on the calendar; more than one business
        # there fits. Ask with just those — Places ranking would bury them (the
        # right shop was once one of 18 within 35 m).
        opts = [{"id": c.get("place_id") or c["name"], "label": c["name"],
                 "sublabel": f"at {cal.get('address')}"} for c in cal["shortlist"]]
        if lodging():
            opts.append(_lodging_choice(lodging()))
        opts.append({"id": "__other__", "label": "Somewhere else", "value": OTHER_VALUE,
                     "sublabel": "type it instead"})
        ctx = out.get("_ctx") or ""
        out.update(verdict="choose", confidence="calendar-address", certainty=0.7,
                   question="Which of these was it?",
                   prompt=(f"{ctx} — which of these was it?" if ctx else "Which of these was it?"),
                   options=opts, default_id=None,
                   reason=f"at the address on the user's calendar ({cal.get('address')}); "
                          f"{len(cal['shortlist'])} businesses there fit")
        return out
    cal_name = cal.get("name") if cal else None
    if cal_name:
        return _resolve(out, cal_name, "calendar",
                        f"on the user's calendar at this spot during this window")

    # 3. Lodging — where the user SLEEPS is not a venue, and no Places lookup
    # can return it. A rented flat produced a design studio, a shop and a linen
    # store: all real businesses at that address, none of them where the user
    # was. Offering that list is worse than useless, because the default would
    # then write a shop into the user's day.
    #
    # A six-hour stay is the tell. No café, shop or clinic holds anyone that
    # long; somewhere that does is somewhere slept in. Timezone-free on purpose
    # — a duration means the same thing in every zone, and travel changes zones.
    nights, sleep_names = sleeps_here(lat, lon, key, exclude_id=visit["id"])
    # The booking is the lodging's name. Without it, eleven confirmations at a
    # rented flat under four spellings (three variants of "the rental" and a
    # bakery 8 m off) could never agree, so every return home fell to
    # `lodging-inferred` and asked "were you at the rental?" — whose own "Yes"
    # stored a fifth spelling. One night here is enough when travel_schedule
    # says the user is booked here that date.
    booked = scheduled_lodging((arr.astimezone(tz) if tz else arr).date()) if nights >= 1 else None
    if booked:
        return _resolve(out, booked, "lodging-booked",
                        f"{nights} stays over {SLEEP_STAY_HOURS:.0f}h here, and the trip is "
                        f"booked at {booked} for this date")
    if nights >= SLEEP_MIN_NIGHTS:
        # A confirmed name only counts as the LODGING'S name if it was confirmed
        # for a stay long enough to have been the night. Any other confirmation
        # nearby is a neighbour.
        if len(sleep_names) == 1:
            return _resolve(out, sleep_names[0], "lodging-known",
                            f"confirmed on a stay over {SLEEP_STAY_HOURS:.0f}h here "
                            f"({nights} such nights)")
        return _resolve(out, CFG.get("lodging_label") or "the rental", "lodging-inferred",
                        f"{nights} stays over {SLEEP_STAY_HOURS:.0f}h here — the user sleeps at "
                        f"this spot, but no long stay carries a confirmed name")

    # 3.5. A night nothing above could name: not the booking by its nights or
    # its address, not the user's own answers here, not a bed slept in before.
    # If nothing places the booking elsewhere either, ask about the booking
    # itself, one tap, rather than offer a café list: no café holds anyone
    # till morning. See LODGING_NEAR_M.
    if is_night:
        stay = lodging()
        if stay:
            return _resolve(out, stay["name"], "lodging-night", stay["reason"])

    # 4. Density — one plausible venue, or a tie that only a tap can break.
    try:
        from places_nearby import nearby
        cands = nearby(lat, lon, radius=int(ASK_RADIUS_M), max_results=20)
    except Exception:
        cands = []
    if not cands:
        cands = _osm_fallback_candidates(lat, lon, ASK_RADIUS_M)
    plausible = [c for c in cands
                 if not ((set(c.get("types") or []) | {c.get("primary_type") or ""})
                         & (DEMOTED_TYPES | ADDRESS_TYPES))]
    # A destination prior, because distance and review count alone default a
    # 23-minute morning coffee to a laundry (22m) over a coffee shop (20m). Somewhere you SPEND 15+ minutes is
    # overwhelmingly somewhere that serves you something or sells you something,
    # not a government office, a clinic or a dry cleaner sharing the doorway.
    meal = _meal_kinds(arr, mins, tz)
    def prior(c):
        t = set(c.get("types") or []) | {c.get("primary_type") or ""}
        if meal and _serves(t, meal):
            return -1
        if t & DESTINATION_TYPES:
            return 0
        if t & SERVICE_TYPES:
            return 2
        return 1
    plausible.sort(key=lambda c: (prior(c), round(c["distance_m"] / 20),
                                  -(c.get("reviews") or 0)))
    out["n_candidates"] = len(plausible)
    if not plausible:
        # `options`, not `choices` — `segment-ask` (the only consumer) skips
        # any row where `not r.get("options")`, so this verdict has never
        # actually reached the user: it claimed "ask" but carried nothing to
        # ask WITH. A real, if minimal, option restores that — one tap to say
        # so rather than a silently dropped question.
        # Where the user is staying is the one named answer left, and with no rival
        # it is the likeliest.
        stay = lodging()
        out.update(verdict="ask", reason=f"no named venue within {ASK_RADIUS_M:.0f}m"
                                         + (f"; the booking: {stay['reason']}" if stay else ""),
                   question="Where was this?",
                   prompt=(f"{ctx} — where was this?" if ctx else "Where was this?"),
                   options=([_lodging_choice(stay)] if stay else [])
                   + [{"id": "__other__", "label": "Type it in", "value": OTHER_VALUE,
                       "sublabel": "no nearby venue found"}],
                   default_id="__lodging__" if stay else None)
        return out
    if len(plausible) == 1:
        c = plausible[0]
        return _resolve(out, c["name"], "singular",
                        f"the only venue within {ASK_RADIUS_M:.0f}m ({c['distance_m']:.0f}m)")

    # Where the user is staying goes first, in place of the fourth venue: within
    # LODGING_NEAR_M of the flat it is the likelier answer, and no venue is
    # then decisively ahead of it, so the card carries no default.
    stay = lodging()
    opts = ([_lodging_choice(stay)] if stay else []) + [
        {"id": c.get("place_id") or c["name"], "label": c["name"],
         "sublabel": f"{round(c['distance_m'])}m · {c.get('type_display') or c.get('primary_type') or ''}".strip(" ·"),
         "distance_m": round(c["distance_m"])}
        for c in plausible[:MAX_CHOICES - (1 if stay else 0)]]
    opts.append({"id": "__other__", "label": "Somewhere else", "value": OTHER_VALUE,
                 "sublabel": "type it instead"})
    decisive = not stay and (len(plausible) == 1 or (
        prior(plausible[0]) < prior(plausible[1])
        or plausible[1]["distance_m"] - plausible[0]["distance_m"] >= 10))
    # One tap for "this wasn't a stop at all" — a walk past a café is
    # otherwise forced into naming it. Its value is what `findme resolve`
    # already reads as a withdrawal (non-stop), never a place.
    opts.append({"id": "__passing__", "label": "Just passing through", "value": "not a stop",
                 "sublabel": "not a stop"})
    out.update(verdict="choose", question="Which of these was it?",
               prompt=(f"{ctx} — which of these was it?" if ctx
                       else "Which of these was it?"),
               options=opts,
               # The ranked top is the default when it is decisively ahead — the
               # same judgement the list already makes. When it is not, no
               # default: see the note by DESTINATION_TYPES.
               default_id=opts[0]["id"] if decisive else None,
               reason=f"{len(plausible)} plausible venues within {ASK_RADIUS_M:.0f}m — distance cannot separate them"
                      + (f"; and the booking: {stay['reason']}" if stay else ""))
    return out


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    ap.add_argument("date", nargs="?")
    ap.add_argument("--tz", default=places_local.instance().get("tz") or "UTC")
    ap.add_argument("--visit")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    args = ap.parse_args()
    key = load_key()
    saved = load_saved()

    if args.visit:
        visits = _get(f"location_history?id=eq.{urllib.parse.quote(args.visit)}&select=*", key)
    else:
        if not args.date:
            ap.error("need a date or --visit")
        tz = ZoneInfo(args.tz)
        d0 = datetime.strptime(args.date, "%Y-%m-%d").replace(tzinfo=tz)
        d1 = d0 + timedelta(days=1)
        visits = _get(
            "location_history?segment_type=eq.visit&departed_at=not.is.null"
            f"&arrived_at=gte.{urllib.parse.quote(d0.astimezone(timezone.utc).isoformat())}"
            f"&arrived_at=lt.{urllib.parse.quote(d1.astimezone(timezone.utc).isoformat())}"
            "&select=*&order=arrived_at.asc", key)

    disp_tz = ZoneInfo(args.tz)
    seen, results, prev_name = set(), [], None
    for v in visits or []:
        k = (v["arrived_at"], v.get("departed_at"), round(v["latitude"], 5))
        if k in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(k)
        r = classify(v, key, saved, tz=disp_tz, prev=prev_name)
        results.append(r)
        # Carry the last stop we can NAME forward, so the next question can say
        # "after <the market>" instead of leaving the user to work out which gap.
        nm = r.get("name") or r.get("stored_name")
        if nm and r.get("verdict") != "below-floor":
            prev_name = nm

    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(results, indent=2, default=str))
        return
    tz = ZoneInfo(args.tz)
    for r in results:
        if r["verdict"] == "below-floor":
            continue
        t = datetime.fromisoformat(r["arrived_at"].replace("Z", "+00:00")).astimezone(tz)
        head = f"{t:%H:%M}  {r['minutes']:>5.0f} min  {r['verdict'].upper()}"
        if r.get("confidence"):
            head += f" [{r['confidence']}]"
        print(head)
        if r.get("prompt"):
            print(f"          \"{r['prompt']}\"")
        print(f"          {r['reason']}")
        for c in r.get("options") or []:
            star = "  ← default" if c["id"] == r.get("default_id") else ""
            sub = f"  ({c['sublabel']})" if c.get("sublabel") else ""
            print(f"            [ {c['label']} ]{sub}{star}")
        if r.get("conflicting_prior_names"):
            print(f"          prior names here disagree: {r['conflicting_prior_names']}")
    shown = [r for r in results if r["verdict"] != "below-floor"]
    skipped = sum(1 for r in shown if r["verdict"] == "skip-known")
    conf = sum(1 for r in shown if r["verdict"] == "confirm")
    defaulted = sum(1 for r in shown if r.get("default_id"))
    print(f"\n{len(shown)} stop(s) over {MIN_STOP_MIN:.0f} min — "
          f"{skipped} known (no question), {conf} one-tap confirm, "
          f"{len(shown) - conf - skipped} multi-choice; "
          f"{defaulted} have an applicable default")


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
